IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `hca-dates`
IMPORT `hca-rules`

-- Health Complaints Act 2016 (Vic) -- the questions of the PERSON WHO RECEIVED OR SOUGHT A HEALTH SERVICE and wants to
-- complain about it, and of the others the Act lets complain: another person on their behalf (s 6) and their carer
-- (s 7). The Act's terms with plain glosses; the Commissioner's judgments are inputs. Each question takes only plain
-- facts (yes/no, numbers, dates, a choice from a list); the shared definitions, the code of conduct clauses and the
-- penalty table are in hca-rules.l4.
--
-- Where a section is used by one question only, its words are applied here, with the quotation.
--
-- UNOFFICIAL VERSION: quotations from a plain-text conversion by Legalese Commons of the Word file of the Health
-- Complaints Act 2016, Version No. 011 (22 October 2025), not the authorised version. (c) State of Victoria; published
-- with the authority of the Government Printer for the State of Victoria. No part may be reproduced except in accordance
-- with the Copyright Act 1968 (Cth). Quoted under the owner's ruling of 24 October 2026 (SOURCE-LICENSE.md).
-- BASELINE ENCODING. Not for public use as advice. See NOTES.md.

§ `Health Complaints Act 2016 -- the person who complains`

§§ `Is it a health service`

@export Was what I received or sought a "health service" under the Health Complaints Act 2016 (Vic) s 3? Only a health service can be complained about under the Act. It covers activities to assess, maintain or improve health or to diagnose, prevent or treat illness, injury or disability (as intended or claimed), disability, palliative and aged care, surgery, prescribing and dispensing, health education, therapeutic counselling, support services, ancillary services such as laundry or catering that affect patients, and prescribed services.
GIVEN `what kind of service was it` IS A `Kind of service` @desc The kind of service: an activity meant or claimed to assess, maintain or improve your physical, mental or psychological health or to diagnose, prevent or treat an illness, injury or disability; a health related disability, palliative care or aged care service; a surgical or related service; prescribing or dispensing a drug or medicinal preparation; prescribing or dispensing an aid or equipment for therapeutic use; health education; therapeutic counselling or psychotherapy; a support service needed to carry out one of these; an ancillary service (such as laundry, cleaning or catering); a service prescribed by regulations; or none of these
      `does the ancillary service affect patients` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For an ancillary service only: whether it affects or may affect people who are receiving health services (answer no otherwise)
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`Was it a health service` MEANS
    `s 3 -- a health service` `what kind of service was it` `does the ancillary service affect patients`

@export Was it a "general health service" under the Health Complaints Act 2016 (Vic) s 3 -- a health service that is not the practice of a health profession registered under the Health Practitioner Regulation National Law (for example naturopathy, massage, counselling, cosmetic therapies)? The general code of conduct and prohibition orders apply only to general health services.
GIVEN `what kind of service was it` IS A `Kind of service` @desc The kind of service (as for the health service question)
      `does the ancillary service affect patients` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For an ancillary service only: whether it affects or may affect people receiving health services
      `was it the practice of a registered health profession` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the service was the practice of a health profession under the National Law (medicine, nursing and midwifery, dentistry, pharmacy, physiotherapy, psychology, chiropractic, osteopathy, optometry, podiatry, occupational therapy, medical radiation practice, paramedicine, Chinese medicine, Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander health practice); answer no for a registered practitioner doing something outside their profession
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`Was it a general health service` MEANS
    `s 3 -- a general health service` (`s 3 -- a health service` `what kind of service was it` `does the ancillary service affect patients`) `was it the practice of a registered health profession`

§§ `Complaining to the provider`

-- ss 5(1), 6(1), 7(1) "may make a complaint to a health service provider about" the listed heads. A grant limited by its
-- own wording: where no head is met, NOTHING-kind *limited by its own wording* (the Act does not stop a person complaining
-- to a provider about anything else; it gives no right to).
@export Can I make a complaint to the health service provider under the Health Complaints Act 2016 (Vic) ss 5(1), 6(1) and 7(1)? The person who received or sought the service, another person, or a carer may complain about the heads the Act lists.
GIVEN `who am I` IS A `Who is complaining` @desc Who you are: the person who received or sought the service, another person (complaining about a service to someone else), or a carer of the person who received or sought it
      `was the provision unreasonable` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the provider provided the service unreasonably
      `was there an unreasonable failure to provide` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the provider unreasonably failed to provide a service that was sought
      `did the provider fail the health service principles` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the provider unreasonably failed to act consistently with the health service principles of s 4 (access, safety and quality, care and attention, respect and dignity, clear information on treatment, options and costs, inclusive decisions, privacy, a way to comment or complain)
      `was the manner unreasonable` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the service was provided in an unreasonable manner
      `did the provider fail the duty of candour` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the provider failed to comply with the duty of candour under Part 5A of the Health Services Act 1988 (to tell the patient and apologise after a serious adverse patient safety event)
      `is it about how my own complaint was handled` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it is about how the provider dealt with a complaint you made to it, including not meeting the complaint handling standards
      `is it about how the patient's complaint was handled` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For another person or a carer: whether it is about how the provider dealt with the patient's own complaint about the service (unreasonable provision, failure, principles or manner; s 6(1)(e)(ii) does not reach the patient's complaint about the duty of candour or about complaint handling)
      `was I as carer treated unreasonably` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For a carer: whether the provider treated you unreasonably when providing, or failing to provide, the service to the person you care for
      `was I as carer treated unreasonably over candour` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For a carer: whether the provider treated you unreasonably in complying, or failing to comply, with the duty of candour for the person you care for
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can I complain to the provider` MEANS
    IF `a head of ss 5(1), 6(1), 7(1)` `who am I` (`Complaint facts` WITH
            `the provision of the service was unreasonable` IS `was the provision unreasonable`
            `there was an unreasonable failure to provide a service sought` IS `was there an unreasonable failure to provide`
            `there was an unreasonable failure to act consistently with the health service principles` IS `did the provider fail the health service principles`
            `the service was provided in an unreasonable manner` IS `was the manner unreasonable`
            `the provider failed to comply with the duty of candour` IS `did the provider fail the duty of candour`
            `it is about how the provider dealt with my complaint to it` IS `is it about how my own complaint was handled`
            `it is about how the provider dealt with the patient's own complaint` IS `is it about how the patient's complaint was handled`
            `the provider treated me unreasonably as carer` IS `was I as carer treated unreasonably`
            `the provider treated me unreasonably as carer over the duty of candour` IS `was I as carer treated unreasonably over candour`
            `a general provider failed to comply with a code of conduct or an order` IS FALSE)
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE NOTHING

§§ `Complaining to the Commissioner`

-- ss 5(2), 6(2), 7(2) with s 8. The route and the time limit together; see `Commissioner complaint answer` in hca-rules.l4.
-- No route: NOTHING-kind *limited by its own wording*, said in the answer as "not yet on these facts".
@export Can I complain to the Health Complaints Commissioner now (Health Complaints Act 2016 (Vic) ss 5(2), 6(2), 7(2) and 8)? You may go straight to the Commissioner if complaining to the provider first would be unreasonable or inappropriate, or about a breach of the code of conduct or a prohibition order by an unregistered provider; otherwise after complaining to the provider, if it is not resolved within 3 months or about how the provider handled it. The time limit is 12 months after the service was completed (or sought, if not provided), unless the Commissioner is satisfied the circumstances require dealing with it.
GIVEN `who am I` IS A `Who is complaining` @desc Who you are: the person who received or sought the service, another person, or a carer of that person
      `was the provision unreasonable` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the provider provided the service unreasonably
      `was there an unreasonable failure to provide` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the provider unreasonably failed to provide a service that was sought
      `did the provider fail the health service principles` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the provider unreasonably failed to act consistently with the health service principles of s 4
      `was the manner unreasonable` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the service was provided in an unreasonable manner
      `did the provider fail the duty of candour` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the provider failed to comply with the duty of candour under Part 5A of the Health Services Act 1988
      `is it about how my own complaint was handled` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it is about how the provider dealt with a complaint you made to it
      `is it about how the patient's complaint was handled` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For another person or a carer: whether it is about how the provider dealt with the patient's own complaint about the service (unreasonable provision, failure, principles or manner)
      `was I as carer treated unreasonably` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For a carer: whether the provider treated you unreasonably when providing or failing to provide the service
      `was I as carer treated unreasonably over candour` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For a carer: whether the provider treated you unreasonably over the duty of candour
      `did an unregistered provider breach the code or an order` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a provider of a general health service failed to comply or act consistently with a code of conduct applying to it, or with an interim prohibition order or prohibition order
      `would complaining to the provider first be unreasonable` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it would be unreasonable or inappropriate to resolve the matter by complaining to the provider first
      `did I complain to the provider` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you made a complaint about this to the provider
      `the day I complained to the provider` IS A DATE @desc The day you made the complaint to the provider (any date if you did not)
      `has the provider resolved it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the provider has resolved your complaint
      `the day the provider resolved it` IS A DATE @desc The day it was resolved (any date if it was not)
      `was the service provided` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the health service was provided at all (answer no if you sought it and it was not provided)
      `the day the service was completed` IS A DATE @desc The day the provision of the service was completed (any date if it was not provided)
      `the day I sought the service` IS A DATE @desc The day the service was sought (used only if it was not provided)
      `today` IS A DATE @desc The day you would complain to the Commissioner
      `is the Commissioner satisfied to deal with it late` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For a late complaint: whether the Commissioner is satisfied that the circumstances of the case require dealing with it (s 8(2)); answer no if not known
GIVETH A `Commissioner complaint answer`
`Can I complain to the Commissioner` MEANS
    IF NOT `there is a route`
    THEN `not yet on these facts: the Act gives no route to the Commissioner`
    ELSE IF NOT (`today` `is after` (`s 8(1) -- the last day to complain to the Commissioner` `was the service provided` `the day the service was completed` `the day I sought the service`))
         THEN `yes, you may complain to the Commissioner`
         ELSE IF `is the Commissioner satisfied to deal with it late`
              THEN `out of time, but the Commissioner is satisfied the circumstances require it and may deal with it (s 8(2))`
              ELSE `no, it is more than 12 months after the service (s 8(1))`
    WHERE
        `there is a route` MEANS `ss 5(2), 6(2), 7(2) -- a route to the Commissioner` `who am I` `my complaint` `my route` `today`
        `my complaint` MEANS
            `Complaint facts` WITH
                `the provision of the service was unreasonable` IS `was the provision unreasonable`
                `there was an unreasonable failure to provide a service sought` IS `was there an unreasonable failure to provide`
                `there was an unreasonable failure to act consistently with the health service principles` IS `did the provider fail the health service principles`
                `the service was provided in an unreasonable manner` IS `was the manner unreasonable`
                `the provider failed to comply with the duty of candour` IS `did the provider fail the duty of candour`
                `it is about how the provider dealt with my complaint to it` IS `is it about how my own complaint was handled`
                `it is about how the provider dealt with the patient's own complaint` IS `is it about how the patient's complaint was handled`
                `the provider treated me unreasonably as carer` IS `was I as carer treated unreasonably`
                `the provider treated me unreasonably as carer over the duty of candour` IS `was I as carer treated unreasonably over candour`
                `a general provider failed to comply with a code of conduct or an order` IS `did an unregistered provider breach the code or an order`
        `my route` MEANS
            `Route facts` WITH
                `it would be unreasonable or inappropriate to complain to the provider first` IS `would complaining to the provider first be unreasonable`
                `I complained to the provider` IS `did I complain to the provider`
                `the day I complained to the provider` IS `the day I complained to the provider`
                `the provider has resolved my complaint` IS `has the provider resolved it`
                `the day the provider resolved it` IS `the day the provider resolved it`

@export By when must I complain to the Health Complaints Commissioner (Health Complaints Act 2016 (Vic) s 8(1))? The last day is 12 months after the provision of the service was completed, or, if it was not provided, 12 months after it was sought. After that the Commissioner may still deal with it if satisfied the circumstances require it (s 8(2)).
GIVEN `was the service provided` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the health service was provided at all
      `the day the service was completed` IS A DATE @desc The day the provision of the service was completed (any date if it was not provided)
      `the day I sought the service` IS A DATE @desc The day the service was sought (used only if it was not provided)
GIVETH A DATE
`By when must I complain to the Commissioner` MEANS
    `s 8(1) -- the last day to complain to the Commissioner` `was the service provided` `the day the service was completed` `the day I sought the service`

-- s 9 "(1) A complaint made to the Commissioner may be made either orally or in writing. (2) A person who makes an oral
-- complaint must confirm the complaint as recorded in writing by the Commissioner under section 10 as soon as
-- practicable. (3) The Commissioner may waive the requirement ..." A written complaint: the "who"-clause unmet, JUST FALSE
-- (ruling (b) of 2026-11-05). Waived: JUST FALSE (the requirement is waived).
@export Must I confirm my complaint in writing (Health Complaints Act 2016 (Vic) s 9)? A complaint to the Commissioner may be oral or written; an oral complaint must be confirmed as the Commissioner recorded it, as soon as practicable, unless the Commissioner waives that.
GIVEN `did I complain orally` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you made your complaint to the Commissioner orally (by phone or in person) rather than in writing
      `has the Commissioner waived confirmation` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Commissioner has waived the requirement to confirm it
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Must I confirm my complaint in writing` MEANS
    `as an answer` (`did I complain orally` AND (NOT `has the Commissioner waived confirmation`))

-- s 11(1): "The Commissioner must give reasonable assistance to a person who is making a complaint to the Commissioner or
-- confirming a complaint made to the Commissioner." Not making or confirming one: NOTHING-kind *limited by its own wording*.
@export Must the Health Complaints Commissioner help me make or confirm my complaint (Health Complaints Act 2016 (Vic) s 11)? Yes, with reasonable assistance, including identifying the parties.
GIVEN `am I making or confirming a complaint to the Commissioner` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are making a complaint to the Commissioner or confirming one you made
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Must the Commissioner help me` MEANS
    IF `am I making or confirming a complaint to the Commissioner` THEN JUST TRUE ELSE NOTHING

§§ `When the Commissioner has the complaint`

@export Am I a "party" to the complaint (Health Complaints Act 2016 (Vic) ss 3 and 22)? The parties are the complainant, the provider complained about, and the person who received or sought the service if they are not the complainant and have not told the Commissioner they do not wish to be a party. Parties are given notices and take part in conciliation.
GIVEN `what is my place in the complaint` IS A `My place in the complaint` @desc Your place: the complainant; the provider complained about; the person who received or sought the service but did not make the complaint; or someone else
      `did I tell the Commissioner I do not wish to be a party` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you notified the Commissioner under s 22 that you do not wish to be a party
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`Am I a party to the complaint` MEANS
    `s 3 -- I am a party to the complaint` `what is my place in the complaint` `did I tell the Commissioner I do not wish to be a party`

-- s 14 "The Commissioner may refuse to deal, or cease to deal, with a complaint made to the Commissioner if -- (a) ... (j)".
-- An "if" condition: none met, JUST FALSE ("s 14 gives no ground on these facts"; ss 15(2), 34(b) and 38(3)(a) give other
-- ways to stop, asked separately or not encoded).
@export Can the Commissioner refuse to deal with, or stop dealing with, my complaint (Health Complaints Act 2016 (Vic) s 14)? Only on the grounds listed: frivolous or lacking substance, bad faith, improper purpose, no reply to a request for information, no prospect of resolution, a matter for a court or tribunal, already resolved by the provider, no reasonable attempt with the provider first, or withdrawn.
GIVEN `is the Commissioner satisfied it is frivolous or lacks substance` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Commissioner is satisfied the complaint is frivolous, vexatious, misconceived, lacking in substance or otherwise does not warrant action
      `is the Commissioner satisfied it is not in good faith` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Commissioner is satisfied the complaint is not made in good faith
      `is the Commissioner satisfied of an improper purpose` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Commissioner is satisfied it is made for an improper purpose
      `did I not give information the Commissioner asked for` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you did not comply with the Commissioner's request for more information under s 12(2)
      `does the Commissioner believe dealing with it is not in the public interest` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Commissioner believes it is not in the public interest to deal with the complaint
      `is the Commissioner satisfied there is no prospect of resolution` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Commissioner is satisfied there is no reasonable prospect of resolving it by a complaint resolution process
      `does the Commissioner believe it should be investigated` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Commissioner believes the complaint should be investigated under Part 4
      `is it within a court's or tribunal's jurisdiction` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the subject matter comes within the jurisdiction of a court, tribunal or other body (for example a claim for damages)
      `is it before a court or tribunal` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the subject matter is before a court, tribunal or other body
      `has a court or tribunal decided it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the subject matter has been determined by a court, tribunal or other body
      `does it raise a new matter the Commissioner believes ought to be dealt with` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If it was determined: whether it raises a new matter that the Commissioner believes the Commissioner ought to deal with
      `is the Commissioner satisfied the provider has resolved it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the provider has taken action that the Commissioner is satisfied has resolved the complaint
      `is the Commissioner satisfied I made no reasonable attempt with the provider` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Commissioner is satisfied that reasonable attempts have not been made to resolve it with the provider and that it is reasonable and appropriate to do so
      `have I withdrawn the complaint` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have withdrawn the complaint
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can the Commissioner refuse my complaint` MEANS
    `as an answer` (
           `is the Commissioner satisfied it is frivolous or lacks substance`
        OR `is the Commissioner satisfied it is not in good faith`
        OR `is the Commissioner satisfied of an improper purpose`
        OR (`did I not give information the Commissioner asked for` AND `does the Commissioner believe dealing with it is not in the public interest`)
        OR (`is the Commissioner satisfied there is no prospect of resolution` AND (NOT `does the Commissioner believe it should be investigated`))
        OR `is it within a court's or tribunal's jurisdiction`
        OR `is it before a court or tribunal`
        OR (`has a court or tribunal decided it` AND (NOT `does it raise a new matter the Commissioner believes ought to be dealt with`))
        OR `is the Commissioner satisfied the provider has resolved it`
        OR `is the Commissioner satisfied I made no reasonable attempt with the provider`
        OR `have I withdrawn the complaint`)

-- s 15(2) "The Commissioner may decide not to deal with a complaint if agreement about the description of the complaint
-- is not reached within 10 business days after the Commissioner seeks the agreement, or any longer period determined by
-- the Commissioner." The period is the longer of 10 and the period determined. Not reached within it: once that many
-- complete business days have passed without agreement (fork F4). Otherwise JUST FALSE ("if").
@export Can the Commissioner decline my complaint because we have not agreed its description (Health Complaints Act 2016 (Vic) s 15(2))? Only if agreement is not reached within 10 business days after the Commissioner seeks it, or any longer period the Commissioner set.
GIVEN `have we agreed the description` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you and the Commissioner have agreed the formal description of your complaint
      `business days since the Commissioner sought agreement` IS A NUMBER @desc The number of complete business days (not weekends or public holidays) that have passed since the Commissioner sought your agreement to the description
      `the longer period the Commissioner set` IS A NUMBER @desc A longer period, in business days, that the Commissioner determined; 0 if none
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can the Commissioner decline for want of an agreed description` MEANS
    `as an answer` ((NOT `have we agreed the description`) AND (`business days since the Commissioner sought agreement` AT LEAST (max 10 `the longer period the Commissioner set`)))

-- s 21(1) "The Commissioner may decide to deal with a complaint in respect of which the Commissioner made a decision under
-- section 14 if -- (a) the Commissioner becomes aware of new information in respect of that complaint; or (b) the
-- Commissioner reasonably believes that further action should be taken". "If": unmet, JUST FALSE. Not a refused
-- complaint: NOTHING-kind *limited by its own wording*.
@export Can the Commissioner take up my complaint again after refusing or stopping it (Health Complaints Act 2016 (Vic) s 21)? Yes, if the Commissioner becomes aware of new information or reasonably believes further action should be taken.
GIVEN `did the Commissioner refuse or stop dealing with it under s 14` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Commissioner refused to deal with, or stopped dealing with, your complaint under s 14
      `is there new information` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Commissioner has become aware of new information about the complaint
      `does the Commissioner believe further action should be taken` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Commissioner reasonably believes further action should be taken
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can the Commissioner take up my complaint again` MEANS
    IF `did the Commissioner refuse or stop dealing with it under s 14`
    THEN `as an answer` (`is there new information` OR `does the Commissioner believe further action should be taken`)
    ELSE NOTHING

-- s 23 "A complainant may withdraw a complaint at any time after making it by written notice given to the Commissioner."
-- s 24 "If a complaint has been withdrawn the Commissioner may proceed with the complaint under this Act if -- (a) the
-- Commissioner reasonably believes that it is in the public interest to do so; or (b) ... the complaint involves -- (i) a
-- contravention of an interim prohibition order or a prohibition order by a general health service provider; or (ii) a
-- contravention by a general health service provider of a code of conduct ...; or (c) ... the complaint may have been
-- withdrawn because of victimisation, coercion, duress or intimidation." "If": none, JUST FALSE.
@export If I withdraw my complaint, can the Commissioner still go on with it (Health Complaints Act 2016 (Vic) ss 23 and 24)? You may withdraw at any time by written notice, but the Commissioner may proceed if it is in the public interest, if it involves an unregistered provider breaching the code of conduct or a prohibition order, or if it may have been withdrawn because of victimisation, coercion, duress or intimidation.
GIVEN `does the Commissioner believe going on is in the public interest` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Commissioner reasonably believes it is in the public interest to proceed
      `does the Commissioner believe it involves an order breach` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Commissioner reasonably believes it involves a general health service provider breaching an interim prohibition order or prohibition order
      `does the Commissioner believe it involves a code breach` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Commissioner reasonably believes it involves a general health service provider breaching a code of conduct
      `does the Commissioner believe I was pressured to withdraw` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Commissioner reasonably believes the complaint may have been withdrawn because of victimisation, coercion, duress or intimidation
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can the Commissioner go on after I withdraw` MEANS
    `as an answer` (`does the Commissioner believe going on is in the public interest` OR `does the Commissioner believe it involves an order breach` OR `does the Commissioner believe it involves a code breach` OR `does the Commissioner believe I was pressured to withdraw`)

DECLARE `Referral answer` IS ONE OF
    `it must be referred to the body that deals with it under that other law, and you must be told (ss 25, 28(1))`
    `it must be referred to Ahpra or the National Board, and you must be told (ss 26(2), 28(2))`
    `no referral on these facts`

-- s 25: "(1) This section applies if the subject matter of a complaint or a part of a complaint made to the Commissioner
-- would also be a reason for making a complaint or conducting an investigation or inquiry under a relevant law. (2) As soon
-- as practicable after the complaint is made, the Commissioner must refer the complaint or the part ..." "relevant law"
-- (s 3): the Disability Act 2006, the Health Records Act 2001, the Privacy and Data Protection Act 2014, the Mental Health
-- and Wellbeing Act 2022, the National Disability Insurance Scheme Act 2013 (Cth). s 26(2): "If it is agreed under section
-- 150 of the National Law to deal with the complaint or a part of the complaint under that Law, the Commissioner must
-- refer the complaint to [Ahpra] or the relevant National Board." s 28: the complainant is notified of either referral.
-- Neither: "no referral" (the Commissioner may still divide or refer under s 19(1)(b), a discretion, not encoded). s 27:
-- a code-of-conduct part stays with the Commissioner as well.
@export Must the Commissioner refer my complaint to another body (Health Complaints Act 2016 (Vic) ss 25, 26 and 28)? Yes if it could also be complained about under the disability, health records, privacy, mental health or NDIS laws, or if it is agreed under the National Law that Ahpra or a National Board deals with it; you must be told.
GIVEN `could it be complained about under a relevant law` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the subject matter (or part of it) would also be a reason for a complaint, investigation or inquiry under the Disability Act 2006, the Health Records Act 2001, the Privacy and Data Protection Act 2014, the Mental Health and Wellbeing Act 2022 or the National Disability Insurance Scheme Act 2013 (Cth)
      `was it agreed under s 150 of the National Law to deal with it there` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it was agreed under s 150 of the Health Practitioner Regulation National Law (between the Commissioner and the National Board) to deal with the complaint or part of it under that Law
GIVETH A `Referral answer`
`Must my complaint be referred` MEANS
    IF `could it be complained about under a relevant law`
    THEN `it must be referred to the body that deals with it under that other law, and you must be told (ss 25, 28(1))`
    ELSE IF `was it agreed under s 150 of the National Law to deal with it there`
         THEN `it must be referred to Ahpra or the National Board, and you must be told (ss 26(2), 28(2))`
         ELSE `no referral on these facts`

-- Division 4 of Part 2, deferral of notifications. s 29(1): the Commissioner "may give a relevant notice or information to
-- a party ... at the same time as taking a relevant action" (a search warrant, a general health service warning
-- statement, the service of an interim prohibition order) if the Commissioner reasonably believes earlier advice may
-- prejudice an investigation or place a person or the public at serious risk; (3) "must give [it] ... without delay if the
-- Commissioner ceases to hold the belief" first. s 30: at a National Board's request on a reasonable belief, "for a
-- specified period which must not exceed the relevant period" (until the Board advises it no longer holds the belief).
-- s 31: the same at the Disability Services Commissioner's request; the relevant period ends at the first of (a) its
-- advice, (b) the end of its investigation, (c) "the end of the period of 6 months following the receipt of the complaint
-- by the Disability Services Commissioner" (the day 6 months after receipt, fork F2). Held back: JUST TRUE; otherwise the
-- notice is due, JUST FALSE. Not a "relevant notice or information" (s 3): NOTHING-kind *limited by its own wording*.
@export Can the Commissioner hold back from me a notice or a copy of the complaint I would otherwise be given (Health Complaints Act 2016 (Vic) ss 29 to 31)? Only for listed notices, and only while the Commissioner believes giving it earlier may prejudice an investigation or put someone at serious risk (until a search, warning statement or interim order), or for a period a National Board or the Disability Services Commissioner asked for.
GIVEN `is it a notice the Act lets be held back` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it is a "relevant notice or information": a copy of the formal description of the complaint (s 17), or a notice or information under ss 13(3), 19(3), 20(3), 21(2), 28(1)(a), (c), (d) or (2), 49(1) or (3)
      `does the Commissioner believe early notice may prejudice an investigation or risk harm` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Commissioner reasonably believes (and still believes) that advising you earlier may prejudice an investigation or place the life, health, safety or welfare of a person or the public at serious risk
      `has the search, warning statement or interim order been taken yet` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the relevant action (executing a search warrant, publishing a general health service warning statement, or serving an interim prohibition order) has already been taken
      `did a National Board ask and the Commissioner decide to hold it back` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a National Board, on a reasonable belief of prejudice or risk, asked the Commissioner not to give it, and the Commissioner decided not to for a specified period
      `has the National Board said it no longer holds that belief` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the National Board has advised the Commissioner that it no longer holds that belief
      `did the Disability Services Commissioner ask and the Commissioner decide to hold it back` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Disability Services Commissioner asked the Commissioner not to give it, and the Commissioner decided not to for a specified period
      `has the Disability Services Commissioner withdrawn its view or ended its investigation` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Disability Services Commissioner has advised it no longer holds its view, or its investigation under the Disability Act 2006 has ended
      `the day the Disability Services Commissioner received the complaint` IS A DATE @desc The day the Disability Services Commissioner received the complaint (any date if it did not)
      `has the period the Commissioner specified ended` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the period the Commissioner specified for holding it back (under s 30 or 31) has ended
      `today` IS A DATE @desc Today
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can the Commissioner hold back my notice` MEANS
    IF NOT `is it a notice the Act lets be held back`
    THEN NOTHING
    ELSE `as an answer` (`s 29 holds it` OR `s 30 holds it` OR `s 31 holds it`)
    WHERE
        `s 29 holds it` MEANS `does the Commissioner believe early notice may prejudice an investigation or risk harm` AND (NOT `has the search, warning statement or interim order been taken yet`)
        `s 30 holds it` MEANS `did a National Board ask and the Commissioner decide to hold it back` AND (NOT `has the National Board said it no longer holds that belief`) AND (NOT `has the period the Commissioner specified ended`)
        `s 31 holds it` MEANS
                `did the Disability Services Commissioner ask and the Commissioner decide to hold it back`
            AND (NOT `has the Disability Services Commissioner withdrawn its view or ended its investigation`)
            AND (NOT (`today` `is after` (`the date` 6 `months after` `the day the Disability Services Commissioner received the complaint`)))
            AND (NOT `has the period the Commissioner specified ended`)

§§ `Complaint resolution and conciliation`

-- s 40 "The Commissioner must not commence a conciliation of a complaint unless -- (a) the parties have agreed to
-- participate in the conciliation; and (b) written notice of the conciliation was given to a party under section 33 or 37
-- before the party agreed to participate in the conciliation." "Must not ... unless": unmet, JUST FALSE.
@export Can a conciliation of my complaint begin (Health Complaints Act 2016 (Vic) s 40)? Only if all the parties have agreed to take part, and each was given the written notice of the conciliation before agreeing.
GIVEN `have all the parties agreed to conciliation` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether all the parties (you, the provider and, if not you, the person who received the service) have agreed to take part in the conciliation
      `was each party given written notice before agreeing` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether each party was given the written notice of the conciliation (under s 33 or 37: date, scope, the Commissioner's powers, the parties' obligations and the offences) before agreeing
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can conciliation begin` MEANS
    `as an answer` (`have all the parties agreed to conciliation` AND `was each party given written notice before agreeing`)

DECLARE `Conciliation material` IS ONE OF
    `something said or done in the conciliation`
    `an agreement reached in the conciliation`
    `an undertaking the provider gave the Commissioner`

-- s 43 "(1) Subject to subsection (2), a party must not disclose anything said or done in a conciliation or any agreement
-- reached in a conciliation outside the process. Penalty: ... 20 penalty units ... (2) A party may disclose anything said
-- or done in a conciliation outside the process with the consent of the person to whom the information relates. ... (4)
-- This section does not apply to an undertaking given under section 38(2) in the course of a conciliation." Forbidden:
-- JUST TRUE. Consent reaches only "anything said or done", not "any agreement reached" (finding 6). An undertaking: s 43
-- does not apply, JUST FALSE. Not a party: NOTHING-kind *limited by its own wording* (s 43 binds parties).
@export Am I forbidden to tell others what was said, done or agreed in the conciliation (Health Complaints Act 2016 (Vic) s 43)? A party must not disclose it outside the process (20 penalty units), except something said or done with the consent of the person it is about; a provider's undertaking is not covered.
GIVEN `am I a party` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are a party to the complaint (the complainant, the provider, or the person who received the service)
      `what would I disclose` IS A `Conciliation material` @desc What you would disclose: something said or done in the conciliation, an agreement reached in it, or an undertaking the provider gave the Commissioner (s 38(2))
      `does the person it is about consent` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person to whom the information relates consents to the disclosure
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Am I forbidden to disclose the conciliation` MEANS
    IF NOT `am I a party`
    THEN NOTHING
    ELSE CONSIDER `what would I disclose`
         WHEN `something said or done in the conciliation` THEN `as an answer` (NOT `does the person it is about consent`)
         WHEN `an agreement reached in the conciliation` THEN JUST TRUE
         WHEN `an undertaking the provider gave the Commissioner` THEN JUST FALSE
         OTHERWISE NOTHING

-- s 43(3) "Evidence of anything said or done in a conciliation is not admissible in any hearing or proceeding in a court or
-- a tribunal." A flat negative: JUST FALSE. An agreement or undertaking: s 43(3) speaks of "anything said or done"
-- (NOTHING-kind *silent*; s 43(4) takes undertakings out of s 43 altogether).
@export Can what was said or done in the conciliation be used as evidence in a court or tribunal, for example in a negligence claim (Health Complaints Act 2016 (Vic) s 43(3))? No: it is not admissible.
GIVEN `what would be used` IS A `Conciliation material` @desc What would be used as evidence: something said or done in the conciliation, an agreement reached in it, or a provider's undertaking
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can the conciliation be used in court` MEANS
    CONSIDER `what would be used`
    WHEN `something said or done in the conciliation` THEN JUST FALSE
    OTHERWISE NOTHING

DECLARE `End of the process` IS ONE OF
    `a copy of the written record of the agreement (s 38(1))`
    `written notice of the reasons for taking no further action (s 38(4))`
    `written notice of the investigation, with the formal description of the complaint (s 49)`
    `nothing yet: the Commissioner has not decided what follows (s 38(3))`

DECLARE `What the Commissioner decided` IS ONE OF
    `to take no further action`
    `to investigate`
    `not yet decided`

-- s 38 "(1) If agreement is reached ... the Commissioner must -- (a) make a written record of the agreement; and (b) give a
-- copy of the written record to each party. ... (3) If agreement ... is not reached ..., the Commissioner may -- (a) decide
-- to take no further action ...; or (b) conduct an investigation under Part 4 ... (4) If the Commissioner makes a decision
-- under subsection (3)(a), the Commissioner must give written notice of the reasons for that decision to the parties."
-- s 49(1), (2): notice of a complaint investigation to the parties, with the formal description. For a party.
@export What must I be given when the complaint resolution process ends (Health Complaints Act 2016 (Vic) ss 38 and 49)? If agreement is reached, a copy of the written record of it; if not and the Commissioner takes no further action, written reasons; if the Commissioner investigates, written notice of the investigation.
GIVEN `was agreement reached` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether agreement was reached on resolving the complaint
      `what did the Commissioner decide` IS A `What the Commissioner decided` @desc If no agreement: whether the Commissioner decided to take no further action, to investigate, or has not yet decided
GIVETH A `End of the process`
`What must I be given at the end of the process` MEANS
    IF `was agreement reached`
    THEN `a copy of the written record of the agreement (s 38(1))`
    ELSE CONSIDER `what did the Commissioner decide`
         WHEN `to take no further action` THEN `written notice of the reasons for taking no further action (s 38(4))`
         WHEN `to investigate` THEN `written notice of the investigation, with the formal description of the complaint (s 49)`
         OTHERWISE `nothing yet: the Commissioner has not decided what follows (s 38(3))`

-- s 44 "On the completion of a conciliation the Commissioner must provide written notice to the parties that specifies --
-- (a) the date the conciliation ceased; and (b) the outcome of the conciliation." Not completed: JUST FALSE ("On").
@export Must I be told in writing when the conciliation ended and how it came out (Health Complaints Act 2016 (Vic) s 44)? Yes, every party, once the conciliation is complete.
GIVEN `is the conciliation complete` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the conciliation has been completed
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Must I be told the conciliation outcome` MEANS
    `as an answer` `is the conciliation complete`

§§ `Investigation`

-- s 45 "The Commissioner may conduct an investigation of a complaint made to the Commissioner, if the Commissioner
-- reasonably believes that the complaint should be investigated and -- (a) the complaint is not suitable for a complaint
-- resolution process; or (b) a complaint resolution process dealing with the complaint has not been successful; or (c) a
-- health service provider who is a party ..., without reasonable excuse, fails to participate in a complaint resolution
-- process; or (d) a health service provider who is a party ... does not comply with -- (i) a request for more information
-- under section 18; or (ii) a notice requiring ... a written response under section 35; or (iii) a notice requiring the
-- production of a document or other evidence under section 41; or (e) a general health service provider has contravened
-- a code of conduct applying to the general health service." "If": unmet, JUST FALSE.
@export Can the Commissioner investigate my complaint (Health Complaints Act 2016 (Vic) s 45)? If the Commissioner reasonably believes it should be investigated and it is unsuitable for, or failed in, a complaint resolution process, or the provider would not take part or did not answer the Commissioner's request, notice or demand for documents, or an unregistered provider breached the code of conduct.
GIVEN `does the Commissioner believe it should be investigated` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Commissioner reasonably believes the complaint should be investigated
      `is it unsuitable for a complaint resolution process` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the complaint is not suitable for a complaint resolution process
      `did a complaint resolution process fail` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a complaint resolution process on it has not been successful
      `did the provider refuse to take part without excuse` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the provider, without reasonable excuse, failed to take part in a complaint resolution process
      `did the provider not answer a request, notice or demand for documents` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the provider did not comply with a request for information (s 18), a notice to respond (s 35) or a notice to produce documents (s 41)
      `did an unregistered provider breach the code` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a general health service provider has contravened a code of conduct applying to the general health service
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can the Commissioner investigate my complaint` MEANS
    `as an answer` (`does the Commissioner believe it should be investigated` AND (`is it unsuitable for a complaint resolution process` OR `did a complaint resolution process fail` OR `did the provider refuse to take part without excuse` OR `did the provider not answer a request, notice or demand for documents` OR `did an unregistered provider breach the code`))

-- s 61(1) "If the Commissioner decides to conduct a hearing in an investigation under Part 4, the Commissioner must at least
-- 10 business days before the date on which the hearing is to commence give written notice of the hearing -- (a) for a
-- complaint investigation, to the parties to the complaint". Counted as the complete business days after the day of the
-- notice and before the hearing day (fork F5). A party to a complaint investigation only: otherwise NOTHING-kind *limited
-- by its own wording*.
@export Was I given enough notice of the Commissioner's investigation hearing (Health Complaints Act 2016 (Vic) s 61)? Each party to a complaint investigation must be given written notice of the date and place at least 10 business days before the hearing.
GIVEN `am I a party to the complaint investigation` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are a party to the complaint being investigated
      `business days between the notice and the hearing` IS A NUMBER @desc The number of business days (not weekends or public holidays) after the day you were given written notice and before the day the hearing begins
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Was I given enough notice of the hearing` MEANS
    IF `am I a party to the complaint investigation`
    THEN `as an answer` (`business days between the notice and the hearing` AT LEAST 10)
    ELSE NOTHING

-- s 51(3) "For a complaint investigation, the Commissioner may -- (a) give all or part of the investigation report to the
-- complainant; and (b) if the person who received or sought the health service is not the complainant, give all or part
-- of the investigation report to that person if that person has agreed to be notified about the outcome of the
-- complaint." A discretion: the answer is whether the Act allows it. (b) "if" unmet: JUST FALSE. Others: NOTHING-kind
-- *limited by its own wording*.
@export Can the Commissioner give me the report of the investigation of my complaint (Health Complaints Act 2016 (Vic) s 51(3))? The Commissioner may give all or part of it to the complainant, and to the person who received the service if they agreed to be told the outcome; it is not a right.
GIVEN `what is my place in the complaint` IS A `My place in the complaint` @desc Your place: the complainant; the provider complained about; the person who received or sought the service but did not complain; or someone else
      `did I agree to be told the outcome` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If you received or sought the service but did not complain: whether you agreed to be notified about the outcome
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can I be given the investigation report` MEANS
    CONSIDER `what is my place in the complaint`
    WHEN `the complainant` THEN JUST TRUE
    WHEN `the person who received or sought the service, not the complainant` THEN `as an answer` `did I agree to be told the outcome`
    OTHERWISE NOTHING

§§ `Protections`

-- s 76 "(1) A person who makes a complaint is not personally liable for any loss, damage or injury suffered by another
-- person merely because of the making of the complaint. (2) A person who produces a document or gives any information or
-- evidence to the Commissioner in making a complaint is not personally liable ... merely because of the production of the
-- document or the giving of the information or evidence." Protected: JUST TRUE. A claim resting on more than the
-- complaint: NOTHING-kind *limited by its own wording* (s 76 does not speak to it).
@export Am I protected from being held liable for making my complaint (Health Complaints Act 2016 (Vic) s 76)? A complainant is not personally liable for loss, damage or injury to another merely because of making the complaint, or of giving the Commissioner documents, information or evidence in making it.
GIVEN `did I make a complaint under the Act` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you made a complaint under the Act (to a provider or to the Commissioner)
      `is the claim only about my complaint or what I gave in making it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the loss, damage or injury claimed is said to come merely from your making the complaint, or from documents, information or evidence you gave the Commissioner in making it
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Am I protected for complaining` MEANS
    IF `did I make a complaint under the Act` AND `is the claim only about my complaint or what I gave in making it`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE NOTHING

DECLARE `What was done to me` IS ONE OF
    `threats or intimidation to make me not complain or not continue`
    `refusing to employ me`
    `dismissing me`
    `another detriment`
    `nothing of these kinds`

-- s 80 "(1) A person must not, by threat or intimidation, persuade or attempt to persuade another person not to make a
-- complaint under this Act or not to continue with any process under this Act. ... (2) A person must not refuse to employ,
-- or dismiss another person, or subject another person to any detriment because the other person -- (a) intends to make,
-- makes or has made a complaint under this Act; or (b) intends to take part in, or takes part in, or has taken part in any
-- process under this Act." 60 / 300 penalty units each. An offence on the facts: JUST TRUE. (2) without the "because":
-- JUST FALSE for s 80 ("because" read as "if"). Nothing of these kinds: NOTHING-kind *limited by its own wording*.
@export Is it an offence for someone to treat me this way because of my complaint (Health Complaints Act 2016 (Vic) s 80)? Threatening or intimidating someone not to complain or not to continue, and refusing to employ, dismissing or causing any detriment because a person complains or takes part, are offences (60 penalty units, 300 for a company).
GIVEN `what was done to me` IS A `What was done to me` @desc What was done: threats or intimidation to make you not complain or not continue; refusing to employ you; dismissing you; another detriment; or nothing of these kinds
      `was it because I complained or took part` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it was done because you intended to make, made or had made a complaint under the Act, or intended to take part, took part or had taken part in a process under it
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Is it an offence to treat me so` MEANS
    CONSIDER `what was done to me`
    WHEN `threats or intimidation to make me not complain or not continue` THEN JUST TRUE
    WHEN `nothing of these kinds` THEN NOTHING
    OTHERWISE `as an answer` `was it because I complained or took part`

DECLARE `Representation` IS ONE OF
    `another person who is not a lawyer`
    `an Australian legal practitioner`

-- s 79 "(1) A person may be accompanied or represented by another person in relation to any process under this Act. (2) If
-- the Commissioner so authorises, a person may be represented by an Australian legal practitioner in relation to any
-- process under this Act. (3) A person may be represented by an Australian legal practitioner when the person is -- (a)
-- giving evidence to the Commissioner or an Assistant Commissioner; or (b) producing documents to the Commissioner or an
-- Assistant Commissioner under an investigation hearing notice." A lawyer otherwise: JUST FALSE, reading (2) as limiting
-- (1) for lawyers (fork F6, finding 7).
@export May I be accompanied or represented, and by a lawyer, in the complaint (Health Complaints Act 2016 (Vic) s 79)? Anyone may bring or be represented by another person; a lawyer may represent you if the Commissioner authorises it, or when you give evidence or produce documents under an investigation hearing notice.
GIVEN `who would represent me` IS A `Representation` @desc Who would represent you: another person who is not a lawyer, or an Australian legal practitioner
      `has the Commissioner authorised a lawyer` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Commissioner has authorised you to be represented by a lawyer
      `am I giving evidence or producing documents under a hearing notice` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are giving evidence to the Commissioner or an Assistant Commissioner, or producing documents under an investigation hearing notice
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`May I be represented` MEANS
    CONSIDER `who would represent me`
    WHEN `another person who is not a lawyer` THEN JUST TRUE
    OTHERWISE `as an answer` (`has the Commissioner authorised a lawyer` OR `am I giving evidence or producing documents under a hearing notice`)

-- s 77 "It is a reasonable excuse for a natural person to refuse or fail to give information or do any other thing that
-- the person is required to do by or under Part 2, 3, 4 or 5 or section 129, or by or under a search warrant or
-- investigation hearing notice ... if the giving of the information or the doing of that other thing would tend to
-- incriminate the person." s 78: the same for "a person" where it "would be a breach of legal professional privilege or
-- client legal privilege". Neither: NOTHING-kind *silent* (other reasonable excuses may exist). Not required under those
-- provisions: NOTHING-kind *limited by its own wording*.
@export Is it a reasonable excuse not to give the Commissioner something because it would incriminate me or breach legal privilege (Health Complaints Act 2016 (Vic) ss 77 and 78)? Yes, for anything required under Parts 2 to 5, s 129, a search warrant or a hearing notice: self-incrimination for an individual, legal professional or client legal privilege for anyone.
GIVEN `was I required to under the Act` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are required to give the information or do the thing under Parts 2 to 5 or s 129 of the Act, a search warrant or an investigation hearing notice
      `am I an individual` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are a natural person (not a company)
      `would it tend to incriminate me` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether giving it or doing it would tend to show you committed an offence
      `would it breach legal privilege` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether giving it or doing it would breach legal professional privilege or client legal privilege
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Is it a reasonable excuse for me` MEANS
    IF `was I required to under the Act` AND ((`am I an individual` AND `would it tend to incriminate me`) OR `would it breach legal privilege`)
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE NOTHING

@export What is the most a person can be fined for an offence under the Health Complaints Act 2016 (Vic), in penalty units? For example disclosing what was said in conciliation (s 43), a false statement (s 81), or victimising a complainant (s 80). The dollar value of a penalty unit is set each year under the Monetary Units Act 2004.
GIVEN `which offence` IS AN `Offence` @desc The offence, by section
      `is it a company` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the offender is a body corporate (answer no for an individual)
GIVETH A NUMBER
`What is the most someone can be fined` MEANS
    `the maximum penalty units for` `which offence` `is it a company`

@export What is the longest term of imprisonment for an offence under the Health Complaints Act 2016 (Vic), in months? Only individuals, and only for not complying with an investigation hearing notice (12 months) or a prohibition order, an interim prohibition order or an interstate prohibition (2 years); 0 where none.
GIVEN `which offence` IS AN `Offence` @desc The offence, by section
      `is it a company` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the offender is a body corporate
GIVETH A NUMBER
`What is the longest someone can be imprisoned` MEANS
    `the maximum months of imprisonment for` `which offence` `is it a company`

§§ `The general code of conduct, as the client`

-- Schedule 2, through the clause rules in hca-rules.l4. Each question: JUST FALSE where the service was not a general
-- health service (the code does not reach it); otherwise JUST TRUE where a listed duty was not kept, JUST FALSE where none
-- was, NOTHING where only a "should" clause is engaged (fork F8). A breach is a ground for a complaint to the
-- Commissioner (ss 5(2)(b), 6(2)(b)) and an investigation (s 45(e)).
@export Did the provider break the general code of conduct on safe and ethical practice, consent or informed choice (Health Complaints Act 2016 (Vic) Schedule 2 clauses 1 to 3)? The code applies to general health services: those that are not the practice of a registered health profession.
GIVEN `was it a general health service` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the service was a general health service: a health service that is not the practice of a health profession registered under the National Law
      `was it unsafe or unethical` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the service was not provided in a safe and ethical manner
      `did the provider lack competence` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the provider did not maintain the necessary competence in their field
      `was it outside their training or qualifications` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the service was outside the provider's experience or training, or one they are not qualified to provide
      `did they recommend something I did not need` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether they prescribed or recommended treatments or appliances that did not serve your needs
      `did they fail to refer me on` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether they did not recognise the limits of their treatment and refer you to another competent provider when appropriate
      `did they fail to suggest another opinion` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether they did not recommend that you seek additional opinions or services when appropriate
      `did they fail to help me find other care` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether they did not help you find other appropriate care when required and practicable
      `did they fail to encourage me to tell my doctor` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether they did not encourage you to tell your treating doctor (if any) about the treatment
      `did they fail to warn of interactions` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether they did not advise you of possible adverse interactions with other medicines or treatments they knew or should have known you were taking
      `were they culturally insensitive` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the service was not provided in a culturally sensitive way
      `was proper consent obtained first` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether, before starting, the provider made sure that consent appropriate to the treatment and complying with Victorian law was obtained
      `did they not accept my informed choices` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether they did not accept your right to make informed choices about your health care
      `did they try to talk me out of medical treatment` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether they tried to dissuade you from seeking or continuing medical treatment
      `did they not co-operate with my other providers` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether they did not communicate and co-operate with other providers in your best interests
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Did the provider break the code on safe practice` MEANS
    `the code answer` `was it a general health service` (`as an answer` (
           `cl 1 -- contravened` (`Clause 1 facts` WITH
                `the service was not provided in a safe and ethical manner` IS `was it unsafe or unethical`
                `the provider lacked the necessary competence` IS `did the provider lack competence`
                `the service was outside the provider's experience, training or qualifications` IS `was it outside their training or qualifications`
                `the provider prescribed or recommended something not serving the client's needs` IS `did they recommend something I did not need`
                `the provider did not refer on when that was appropriate` IS `did they fail to refer me on`
                `the provider did not recommend another opinion when appropriate` IS `did they fail to suggest another opinion`
                `the provider did not help find other services when required and practicable` IS `did they fail to help me find other care`
                `the provider did not encourage telling the treating doctor` IS `did they fail to encourage me to tell my doctor`
                `the provider did not advise of possible adverse interactions it knew or should have known of` IS `did they fail to warn of interactions`
                `the service was not culturally sensitive` IS `were they culturally insensitive`)
        OR `cl 2 -- contravened` `was proper consent obtained first`
        OR `cl 3 -- contravened` `did they not accept my informed choices` `did they try to talk me out of medical treatment` `did they not co-operate with my other providers`))

@export Did the provider break the general code of conduct on adverse events, infection control, or practising while ill, impaired or under the influence (Health Complaints Act 2016 (Vic) Schedule 2 clauses 5, 6, 7, 10 and 11)?
GIVEN `was it a general health service` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the service was a general health service (not the practice of a registered health profession)
      `did something go wrong in my treatment` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether an adverse event occurred in the course of your treatment or care
      `did they fail to act promptly to limit the harm` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the provider did not take appropriate and timely measures to minimise harm
      `was there no first aid available` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether appropriate first aid was not available
      `was it serious and no emergency help got` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the event was serious and the provider did not obtain emergency assistance
      `did they not tell me or not fix the cause` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether they did not promptly tell you about the event, or took no remedial steps to reduce the risk of it happening again
      `did they not report it where they should` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether they did not report it to the relevant authority where that was appropriate
      `were infection precautions not taken` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether standard precautions for infection control were not adopted
      `did skin penetration break Victorian law` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether skin penetration or another invasive procedure was done without complying with Victorian law
      `did they have an infectious condition` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the provider had been diagnosed with a medical condition that can be passed on to clients
      `did they put me at risk of it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If so: whether they practised in a way that put you at risk of it
      `did they not follow medical advice on it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If so: whether they did not take and follow advice from a suitably qualified registered practitioner on changing their practice
      `were they under the influence` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether they treated you while under the influence of alcohol or unlawful substances
      `were they impaired by medication` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether they treated you while their capacity was or may have been impaired by prescribed medication
      `were they impaired so as to risk harm` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether they treated you while suffering an impairment, condition or addiction that placed or was likely to place you at risk of harm
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Did the provider break the code on events and health` MEANS
    `the code answer` `was it a general health service` (`as an answer` (
           `cl 5 -- contravened` (`Clause 5 facts` WITH
                `an adverse event occurred` IS `did something go wrong in my treatment`
                `the provider did not take appropriate and timely measures to minimise harm` IS `did they fail to act promptly to limit the harm`
                `appropriate first aid was not available` IS `was there no first aid available`
                `the event was serious and emergency help was not obtained` IS `was it serious and no emergency help got`
                `the event was not promptly disclosed to the client, or no remedial steps were taken` IS `did they not tell me or not fix the cause`
                `it was not reported to the relevant authority where appropriate` IS `did they not report it where they should`)
        OR `cl 6 -- contravened` `were infection precautions not taken` `did skin penetration break Victorian law`
        OR `cl 7, 10, 11 -- contravened` (`Provider's health facts` WITH
                `the provider has a transmissible condition` IS `did they have an infectious condition`
                `the provider practised in a way that put clients at risk of it` IS `did they put me at risk of it`
                `the provider did not take and follow a registered practitioner's advice on it` IS `did they not follow medical advice on it`
                `the provider treated clients under the influence of alcohol or unlawful substances` IS `were they under the influence`
                -- A client cannot know whether the provider sought advice on medication or impairment (cl 10(2), 11(2));
                -- those fields are fixed here and asked in the provider's seat.
                `the provider takes prescribed medication` IS `were they impaired by medication`
                `the provider did not get advice on its effect` IS FALSE
                `the provider treated clients while capacity was or may have been impaired by it` IS `were they impaired by medication`
                `the provider treated clients with an impairment likely to place them at risk of harm` IS `were they impaired so as to risk harm`
                `the provider has an impairment that could place clients at risk` IS FALSE
                `the provider did not seek advice on it` IS FALSE)))

@export Did the provider break the general code of conduct by claiming to cure cancer, making claims it cannot back up, or misleading me about its services or qualifications (Health Complaints Act 2016 (Vic) Schedule 2 clauses 8 and 9)?
GIVEN `was it a general health service` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the service was a general health service (not the practice of a registered health profession)
      `did they claim they can cure cancer or a terminal illness` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether they claimed or represented that they are qualified, able or willing to cure cancer or another terminal illness
      `did they claim to treat cancer symptoms` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether they claimed to be able to treat or relieve the symptoms of cancer or another terminal illness
      `can they back that claim up` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If so: whether they can substantiate that claim
      `did they misinform me` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether they misinformed or misrepresented their products, services, qualifications, training or professional affiliations (including untruthful information about qualifications)
      `did they use a qualification to mislead` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether they used a qualification they hold to mislead you or the public about their competence or ability to treat
      `did they make unprovable claims of effectiveness` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether they claimed, to you or in advertising, that a treatment or service works when that cannot be substantiated
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Did the provider break the code on claims` MEANS
    `the code answer` `was it a general health service` (`as an answer` (`cl 8, 9 -- contravened` (`Claims facts` WITH
        `the provider claimed to be able or willing to cure cancer or a terminal illness` IS `did they claim they can cure cancer or a terminal illness`
        `the provider claimed to treat or relieve symptoms of cancer or a terminal illness` IS `did they claim to treat cancer symptoms`
        `the provider can substantiate that claim` IS `can they back that claim up`
        `the provider misinformed about products, services, qualifications or affiliations` IS `did they misinform me`
        `the provider used a qualification to mislead about competence` IS `did they use a qualification to mislead`
        `the provider made efficacy claims it cannot substantiate` IS `did they make unprovable claims of effectiveness`)))

@export Did the provider break the general code of conduct by exploiting me financially or by sexual misconduct (Health Complaints Act 2016 (Vic) Schedule 2 clauses 12 and 13)?
GIVEN `was it a general health service` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the service was a general health service (not the practice of a registered health profession)
      `did they exploit me financially` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the provider financially exploited you
      `did they give services not meant to help my health` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether they provided services or treatments not designed to maintain or improve your health or wellbeing
      `did referral inducements change hands` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether they accepted or offered financial inducements or gifts as part of referral arrangements with other providers
      `did they ask me for money or gifts` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether they asked you to give, lend or bequeath money or gifts that would benefit them directly or indirectly
      `did they behave sexually or too personally with me` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether they engaged in behaviour of a sexual or close personal nature with you while you were a client
      `did they have a sexual or inappropriate relationship with me` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether they engaged in a sexual or other inappropriate close personal, physical or emotional relationship with you while you were a client
      `did a sexual relationship start too soon after treatment ended` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a sexual relationship began before a reasonable time had passed since the therapeutic relationship ended
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Did the provider break the code on money and conduct` MEANS
    `the code answer` `was it a general health service` (`cl 12, 13 -- contravened` (`Money and conduct facts` WITH
        `the provider financially exploited the client` IS `did they exploit me financially`
        `the provider gave services not designed to maintain or improve health or wellbeing` IS `did they give services not meant to help my health`
        `the provider accepted or offered referral inducements or gifts` IS `did referral inducements change hands`
        `the provider asked the client to give, lend or bequeath money or gifts` IS `did they ask me for money or gifts`
        `the provider engaged in sexual or close personal behaviour with a client` IS `did they behave sexually or too personally with me`
        `the provider had a sexual or inappropriate close relationship with a client` IS `did they have a sexual or inappropriate relationship with me`
        `the provider began a sexual relationship with a former client too soon` IS `did a sexual relationship start too soon after treatment ended`))

@export Did the provider break the general code of conduct on my privacy, my records, insurance, or telling me about the code and how to complain (Health Complaints Act 2016 (Vic) Schedule 2 clauses 14 to 17)?
GIVEN `was it a general health service` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the service was a general health service (not the practice of a registered health profession)
      `did they break privacy law on my health information` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether they did not comply with the privacy laws on your health information (Health Records Act 2001, Privacy and Data Protection Act 2014, Privacy Act 1988)
      `were my records poorly kept` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether they did not keep accurate, legible, up-to-date clinical records of each consultation, held securely
      `did I ask to see my records` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you asked for access to the information in your health records
      `did they not help me see them` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If so: whether they did not take the steps needed to give you access
      `did I or my lawyer ask for my records to be transferred` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you or your legal representative asked for your health record to be transferred
      `did they not transfer them in time` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If so: whether they did not help transfer it in a timely way
      `do they lack indemnity insurance` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether they do not have appropriate indemnity insurance for their practice
      `did they not show me the code and how to complain` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether, when providing or offering the service, they did not bring to your attention or make easily available a copy of the code of conduct and information on how to complain to the Commissioner
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Did the provider break the code on records and information` MEANS
    `the code answer` `was it a general health service` (`cl 14 to 17 -- contravened` (`Records and information facts` WITH
        `the provider broke a privacy law on the client's health information` IS `did they break privacy law on my health information`
        `the provider did not keep accurate, legible, up-to-date and secure records` IS `were my records poorly kept`
        `the client asked for access to their records` IS `did I ask to see my records`
        `the provider did not help with that access` IS `did they not help me see them`
        `the client or their lawyer asked for the records to be transferred` IS `did I or my lawyer ask for my records to be transferred`
        `the provider did not help transfer them in a timely way` IS `did they not transfer them in time`
        `the provider has no appropriate indemnity insurance` IS `do they lack indemnity insurance`
        `the provider did not make the code and the complaints information easily available` IS `did they not show me the code and how to complain`))
